+<h2 id="exec">1. Executive Summary</h2>
+
+<p>This report documents the effort to make the MANDOWN (fall-detection) pipeline correctly recognize a person who has
+fallen <strong>toward or away from the camera</strong> — a "vertical" or "foreshortened" fall, where the body's long axis
+points roughly along the camera's own line of sight instead of across it. This orientation is a fundamentally hard case for
+any 2D-keypoint-based system: a body lying flat toward the camera and a body standing upright both project to a similar
+narrow, vertical shape in the 2D image, so any feature computed purely from 2D pixel angles is structurally blind to the
+difference.</p>
+
+<p>Over this engineering round, the pipeline gained two new structural capabilities that did not exist before:</p>
+<ul>
+  <li><strong>A proper 2D (row + column) ground-plane perspective calibration</strong>, replacing a 1-D (row-only) fit, so
+  expected body size is compared against the right expectation for a person's actual position in frame, not just their
+  vertical position.</li>
+  <li><strong>Monocular 2D→3D pose lifting</strong> (via Google's MediaPipe Pose Landmarker), giving the system a real,
+  camera-orientation-independent estimate of body tilt — something no 2D-only feature can provide by construction.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<p>Alongside these, seven distinct real false positives and false negatives were found through live RTSP testing (not
+synthetic data) and fixed at the root, with two more attempted fixes found unsafe and reverted before shipping once real
+regression testing caught the problem. The system's most safety-critical historical regression point — a specific real
+recorded fall in a validation video — was re-protected and re-verified after every change in this round.</p>
+
+<p>As of this report, the vertical-fall case is <strong>substantially improved and confirmed working for several real
+tested postures</strong> (see §6), but <strong>one further real posture variant (lying with one knee raised) has been found
+still unconfirmed</strong> and remains open — see §7 for the exact numbers and §10 for the recommended next step.</p>
+
+<h2 id="arch">2. System Architecture Overview</h2>
+
+<p>The pipeline is a chain of independent stages. Each stage's output feeds the next; no stage is allowed to silently
+fabricate a value it cannot support with real evidence — this "never fabricate, prefer honest neutral defaults" discipline
+appears repeatedly through the codebase and is treated as a hard design rule.</p>
+
+<pre>Video frame (RTSP / file)
+      │
+      ▼
+┌─────────────────────────┐
+│ YOLO11-pose detector     │  → per-person bounding box + 17 COCO keypoints (x, y, confidence)
+│ + BoT-SORT tracker       │  → a stable track_id per physical person across frames
+└─────────────────────────┘
+      │
+      ├────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
+      ▼                                                          ▼
+┌─────────────────────────┐                         ┌─────────────────────────────┐
+│ Base FeatureExtractor    │                         │ ExtendedFeatureExtractor      │
+│ (5 features, UNCHANGED   │                         │ (torso_angle, hip_angle,      │
+│  since project start)    │                         │  body_axis_angle, leg         │
+│                          │                         │  extension, body_height_norm, │
+│  → feeds the pretrained  │                         │  torso_angle_3d, ...)         │
+│    30-frame LSTM as-is   │                         └─────────────────────────────┘
+└─────────────────────────┘                                       │
+      │                                                            ▼
+      ▼                                            ┌─────────────────────────────┐
+┌─────────────────────────┐                         │ FeatureMandownScorer          │
+│ 5-feature LSTM           │                         │ (down_score, floor_proximity, │
+│ (models/fall_detector.py,│                         │  kneeling_penalty, height_    │
+│  best.pth checkpoint)    │                         │  collapse_cue, persistence,   │
+│  → lstm_mandown_prob     │                         │  pose_quality, fall_motion)   │
+└─────────────────────────┘                         └─────────────────────────────┘
+      │                                                            │
+      └───────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────┘
+                               ▼
+                  ┌─────────────────────────────┐
+                  │ EvidenceFusion                │  → fusion_score, suggested_tier
+                  │ (weighted combination + a     │     (NORMAL / POSSIBLE_MANDOWN / MANDOWN)
+                  │  set of override/cap rules)   │
+                  └─────────────────────────────┘
+                               │
+                               ▼
+                  ┌─────────────────────────────┐
+                  │ TrackStateMachine              │  → per-track hysteresis:
+                  │ (per track_id, sticky states) │     needs N consecutive "up" frames
+                  └─────────────────────────────┘     to confirm, M consecutive
+                               │                        "normal" frames to recover
+                               ▼
+                  ┌─────────────────────────────┐
+                  │ FallDetectionPipeline          │  → drawing, banners, event dedup,
+                  │ (src/pipeline.py)              │     multi-person screenshots, JSON log
+                  └─────────────────────────────┘
+</pre>
+
+<p>Two camera-calibration subsystems run alongside this, continuously refining themselves from real observed people
+(never from a hard-coded assumption about the camera):</p>
+<ul>
+  <li><strong>SceneProfileBuilder</strong> (<code>src/scene_profile.py</code>) — learns, from many confidently-standing
+  people observed over time, how a person's expected pixel height/width varies across the image, and where on-screen the
+  floor actually is.</li>
+  <li><strong>RollingPersonSizeStats</strong> — a simpler, position-agnostic fallback used only until the position-aware
+  fit above has enough samples.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h2 id="logic">3. The MANDOWN Detection Logic, In Detail</h2>
+
+<h3>3.1 Base features (LSTM input) — untouched by design</h3>
+<p>The original 5-feature LSTM classifier (<code>body_angle</code>, <code>cog_height</code>,
+<code>vertical_velocity</code>, <code>bbox_aspect_ratio</code>, <code>keypoint_confidence</code>) and its trained checkpoint
+have been treated as a hard constraint throughout this and prior engineering rounds: never retrained, never given a
+different input contract. Every fix described in this report works entirely in a deterministic geometric layer built
+<em>on top of</em> the LSTM, so the LSTM's ~97-98% test-set accuracy on its original task is never put at risk. The LSTM's
+output (<code>lstm_mandown_prob</code>) is treated as one supporting opinion among several, not a gate — this was a
+deliberate decision from the V3 engineering round, since an already-lying person with a cold (not-yet-filled) or simply
+wrong LSTM buffer must still be detectable from geometry alone.</p>
+
+<h3>3.2 Extended geometric features</h3>
+<p>Computed independently, once per person per frame, from the same 17 keypoints:</p>
+<table>
+<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
+<tr><td><code>torso_angle</code></td><td>Angle of the shoulder-midpoint→hip-midpoint line from vertical, 0 (vertical) to 1 (horizontal). Generalizes correctly to any 2D rotation direction.</td></tr>
+<tr><td><code>hip_angle</code></td><td>Same idea, for the hip-midpoint→knee-midpoint line. Corrupted by knee bend, so used carefully (see §5.7).</td></tr>
+<tr><td><code>body_axis_angle</code></td><td>Long-baseline head→ankle-midpoint axis angle. Corroborates <code>torso_angle</code> only when the torso already shows non-trivial lean (agreement-gated, never a blind override).</td></tr>
+<tr><td><code>leg_extension_ratio</code></td><td>How straight the leg is (hip-knee-ankle path length vs. direct hip-ankle distance). Low = bent knee.</td></tr>
+<tr><td><code>body_height_norm</code></td><td>Observed bounding-box height ÷ this camera's calibrated expected standing height at this exact image position. ~1.0 = normal, well below 1.0 = shorter than a standing person should be here.</td></tr>
+<tr><td><code>cog_height</code></td><td>Normalized hip height in the frame (0 = top, 1 = bottom) — the basis for the self-relative "hip drop" signal (§5.3).</td></tr>
+<tr><td><code>torso_angle_3d</code> <span class="tag tag-info">NEW this round</span></td><td>Angle between the real (lifted) 3D shoulder→hip vector and true vertical. See §5.8.</td></tr>
+</table>
+
+<h3>3.3 FeatureMandownScorer — deterministic geometric scoring</h3>
+<p>Combines the extended features into several independent 0–1 scores:</p>
+<ul>
+  <li><strong><code>down_score</code></strong> — the primary "is this posture down" signal: a blend of body shape
+    (bounding-box aspect ratio), a torso-only orientation cue (a boundary sigmoid past ~36° from vertical, not a linear
+    ramp to 90°), and a calibrated height-collapse cue, each contributing 40/40/20% before an agreement-gated
+    corroboration boost.</li>
+  <li><strong><code>floor_proximity_score</code></strong> — head-to-knee vertical span collapsing, corroborated by the
+    same orientation and height-collapse cues.</li>
+  <li><strong><code>kneeling_penalty</code></strong> — a multiplicative gate (0 = no suppression, 1 = full suppression)
+    applied to both scores above, specifically to stop kneeling/sitting/crouching (bent-leg) postures and — after this
+    round's fixes — several other "this looks down but isn't actually lying" postures from registering as a fall.</li>
+  <li><strong><code>height_collapse_cue</code></strong> — orientation-independent: is the observed body shorter than this
+    camera's calibrated expectation for a standing person at this row/column? Exposed separately because it is the one
+    signal that does not depend on angle at all.</li>
+  <li><strong><code>persistence_score</code></strong> — fraction of the recent window reading consistently "down".</li>
+  <li><strong><code>fall_motion_score</code></strong> — a recent spike in downward velocity/angle-change, supporting
+    evidence only (never required).</li>
+  <li><strong><code>pose_quality_score</code></strong> — mean keypoint confidence, used to discount (never zero out)
+    confidence from an unreliable skeleton.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h3>3.4 EvidenceFusion — combining LSTM + geometry</h3>
+<p>A transparent, weighted rule-based combiner (not a second learned model, by design, so behavior stays debuggable).
+The weighted average of <code>lstm_mandown_prob</code> / <code>feature_confidence</code> / <code>persistence_score</code>
+can be overridden upward by several independent rules, each closing a specific real gap found in production testing:</p>
+<ol>
+  <li>A single very strong signal (LSTM <em>or</em> geometry) is sufficient on its own — recall stays high without
+  requiring both sources to agree.</li>
+  <li><strong>Geometric-alone confirm</strong>: strong, persistent geometric evidence can fully confirm MANDOWN with zero
+  LSTM help — this is what lets an already-lying person with a cold/wrong LSTM still be caught.</li>
+  <li><strong>LSTM-alone safeguard</strong> (mirror-image of #2): a confident LSTM, sustained over many frames, cannot
+  confirm MANDOWN with literally no geometric grounding at all.</li>
+  <li><strong>Moderate-down-score safeguard</strong>: a confident-but-wrong LSTM cannot ride a merely moderate
+  (bend/sit-level) <code>down_score</code> all the way to MANDOWN.</li>
+  <li><strong>Alternate grounding path</strong> (2D): for a foreshortened fall, <code>floor_proximity_score</code> +
+  <code>height_collapse_cue</code> + <code>pose_quality</code> can substitute for a high <code>down_score</code> — <em>only</em>
+  when <code>torso_angle</code> is confirmed near-zero (i.e., genuinely blind, not just unevidenced).</li>
+  <li><strong>3D grounding path</strong> <span class="tag tag-info">NEW this round</span>: a confident
+  <code>torso_angle_3d</code> reading of genuine lying, corroborated by <code>height_collapse_cue</code>, can also
+  substitute — see §5.8-5.9.</li>
+  <li><strong>Direct down_score confirm</strong> <span class="tag tag-info">NEW this round</span>: when
+  <code>down_score</code> alone is already very strong and persistence is sustained, the fusion score is guaranteed to
+  reach the confirm threshold directly, rather than only indirectly through a blend that can dilute it below the
+  threshold on individual frames — see §5.10.</li>
+</ol>
+
+<h3>3.5 TrackStateMachine — temporal hysteresis</h3>
+<p>Per track_id: <code>NORMAL → POSSIBLE_MANDOWN</code> after a few consecutive "up" frames, then
+<code>→ MANDOWN</code> after several more consecutive "up" frames (POSSIBLE_MANDOWN and MANDOWN count identically toward
+this streak). Recovery to <code>NORMAL</code> requires many consecutive genuinely-normal frames — a single flicker never
+clears a real alert. A short detection gap does not reset progress; a long one does.</p>
+
+<h2 id="problem" class="section-break">4. The Vertical/Foreshortened Fall Problem — Why It's Hard</h2>
+
+<p>Every angle-based feature above (<code>torso_angle</code>, <code>hip_angle</code>, <code>body_axis_angle</code>) is
+computed from the 2D projection of keypoints onto the image plane. When a person's body axis points toward or away from
+the camera — lying with their head or feet pointed roughly at the lens — their shoulders and hips project to nearly the
+<em>same</em> (x, y) location, exactly as they would for someone standing upright and facing/facing-away-from the camera.
+There is no lateral spread to measure in either case. This is not a bug in how the angle is computed (the formula itself,
+<code>atan2(|dx|, |dy|)</code>, is mathematically correct for any 2D rotation) — it is a genuine information loss inherent
+to a single 2D projection. No amount of retuning a 2D-only formula can fix it, because the information needed (depth) was
+never captured by that formula's inputs in the first place.</p>
+
+<div class="box box-warn">
+<strong>The practical consequence, observed on real footage this round:</strong> a person lying flat on the ground with
+their body pointed at the camera showed <code>torso_angle ≈ 0.02–0.22</code> — a reading indistinguishable from a
+standing person — while every other geometric signal (<code>down_score</code>, <code>height_collapse_cue</code>) was left
+to do all the work, and in several real postures, still weren't quite enough on their own under the pipeline's
+established, deliberately conservative thresholds.
+</div>
+
+<h2 id="history" class="section-break">5. Full Fix History (Chronological)</h2>
+<p class="small">Each entry names the real trigger (never a hypothetical), the root cause, the fix, and its validated
+outcome. Reverted attempts are kept in the history rather than erased, matching this project's documentation discipline —
+knowing what was tried and why it failed is as valuable as knowing what worked.</p>
+
+<h3>5.1 V4.2 — Height-collapse cue introduced <span class="tag tag-fixed">Kept</span></h3>
+<p>Added <code>height_collapse_cue</code>: is the observed bounding box shorter than expected for a standing person here?
+Driven purely by <code>body_height_norm</code>, never by any angle — the first orientation-independent signal in the
+system. Exposed as a corroboration source for <code>down_score</code>/<code>floor_proximity_score</code> and as an
+alternate grounding path in fusion.</p>
+
+<h3>5.2 V4.3 — Floor-region-based hip check <span class="tag tag-reverted">Reverted</span></h3>
+<p><strong>Idea:</strong> gate <code>height_collapse_cue</code> using a scene-wide <code>FloorRegion</code> (an aggregate
+band of where ankles are typically observed across the whole room), evaluated at the hip position, to exclude a
+standing bend from wrongly triggering the height-collapse-based alternate grounding path.</p>
+<p><strong>Why it was reverted:</strong> found, via real-video regression testing, to be too imprecise for real camera
+perspective — a genuine fall's hip does not reliably land inside an aggregate band that was never designed to be
+perspective-correct for every image column. This caused <strong>two separate real regressions</strong>: it blocked a
+real validation video's true positive when applied too broadly, and even after narrowing its scope, it still blocked a
+newly-reported real vertical-fall case.</p>
+
+<h3>5.3 V4.4 — Per-track, self-relative hip-drop signal <span class="tag tag-fixed">Kept</span></h3>
+<p><strong>Fix:</strong> replaced the scene-wide floor-region check with a signal that needs no scene calibration at all:
+has <em>this specific track's own hip</em> moved down substantially from where it was the last time <em>this same
+track</em> was confidently standing? A track with no prior standing observation (including every "already lying when
+first seen" case) is never gated by this — it simply isn't computed (<code>None</code>), by design, preserving the
+guarantee that a person found already down needs no fall history to be detected.</p>
+<p><strong>A sub-bug found and fixed within this same fix:</strong> using <code>torso_angle &lt; threshold</code> alone
+to decide "is this track confidently standing right now" was not sufficient, because a foreshortened fall <em>also</em>
+reads <code>torso_angle ≈ 0</code> — meaning a track could record its own fallen posture as its "standing" baseline.
+Fixed by also requiring <code>body_height_norm</code> to look genuinely standing-height before trusting a frame as a
+baseline.</p>
+
+<h3>5.4 V4.5 — Fusion alternate-path torso-angle gate <span class="tag tag-fixed">Kept</span></h3>
+<p><strong>Real trigger:</strong> a person bending forward on their feet to reach the ground was confirmed MANDOWN on a
+live camera.</p>
+<p><strong>Root cause:</strong> the "alternate grounding path" (§3.4, rule 5) exists specifically because
+<code>torso_angle</code> is blind for a foreshortened fall — but it never actually verified that <code>torso_angle</code>
+<em>was</em> near-zero. It fired just as easily for a deep bend, where <code>torso_angle</code> is emphatically not blind
+(reads ~0.64) and already carries clear "not down" evidence that <code>down_score</code>/<code>kneeling_penalty</code>
+were supposed to apply.</p>
+<p><strong>Fix:</strong> require <code>torso_angle</code> to be confirmed near-zero (evidence present <em>and</em> below
+a small ceiling) before this path can fire at all.</p>
+
+<h3>5.5 V4.6 — Kneeling-penalty torso-blind fix <span class="tag tag-fixed">Kept</span></h3>
+<p><strong>Real trigger:</strong> a person fallen toward the camera with legs not confidently visible (ankle confidence
+~0.05–0.09) was never flagged.</p>
+<p><strong>Root cause:</strong> <code>kneeling_penalty</code>'s occlusion fallback (used when legs aren't confidently
+visible) also treats a near-zero <code>torso_angle</code> as "confidently upright" — the identical blind spot as §5.4,
+in a different component. Measured on the real miss: this was cutting <code>down_score</code> from ~0.70 down to 0.50.</p>
+<p><strong>Fix:</strong> corroborate with <code>height_collapse_cue</code> the same way the fusion path does — only relent
+the penalty when torso is confirmed near-zero <em>and</em> independent height evidence strongly confirms a genuine
+collapse.</p>
+
+<h3>5.6 V4.7 — Combined-moderate-evidence fusion path <span class="tag tag-reverted">Reverted</span></h3>
+<p><strong>Real trigger:</strong> a person lying flat, legs extended, facing the camera at a shallower angle than the
+near-zero case above (<code>torso_angle=0.22</code>) — too shallow for the existing alternate path, but with
+<code>down_score</code>, <code>floor_proximity_score</code>, and <code>height_collapse_cue</code> all simultaneously
+moderately elevated.</p>
+<p><strong>Idea:</strong> let several independently-moderate, orientation-immune signals combine into confirmation when
+sustained.</p>
+<div class="box box-reverted">
+<strong>Why it was reverted:</strong> a synthetic crouching posture (no real bend, just kneeling penalty near zero)
+measured comparable or higher numbers on the same three signals, and reached MANDOWN under a confident LSTM once this
+path was added — verified before shipping via the project's own negative-control testing discipline. No threshold on
+these three signals alone could admit the real target case without also reopening this crouching false positive.
+</div>
+
+<h3>5.7 V4.8 — Monocular 2D→3D pose lifting <span class="tag tag-fixed">Kept — the structural fix</span></h3>
+<p>This is the headline addition of this round: a new module (<code>src/pose_3d.py</code>) wraps MediaPipe's Pose
+Landmarker to recover an approximate 3D skeleton from the same single 2D image, per detected person, per frame.</p>
+<h4>How it works</h4>
+<ul>
+  <li>Crops the YOLO bounding box (with padding — BlazePose expects to see a full body with margin) and runs MediaPipe's
+  landmarker on just that crop.</li>
+  <li>Reads <code>pose_world_landmarks</code> — a metric, hip-centered 3D estimate. Verified empirically against real
+  footage (not just assumed from documentation) that <strong>Y increases downward</strong> for a normally-oriented
+  camera view: confirmed on a real standing person (torso vector dominated by +Y) and a real, clearly-lying-flat person
+  (angle from the Y axis ~77–86°).</li>
+  <li>Computes <code>torso_angle_3d</code> = angle between the real 3D shoulder→hip vector and vertical, normalized to
+  the same 0–1 convention as the existing 2D <code>torso_angle</code>.</li>
+</ul>
+<h4>Why this is not "just another 2D feature"</h4>
+<p>MediaPipe's estimate is an <em>inference</em> from a learned prior over plausible human body configurations (trained on
+motion-capture datasets), not a physical depth measurement — it does not always agree with 2D features, and its accuracy
+on an unusual camera angle is not guaranteed by documentation alone. This is why every claim about it in this report is
+backed by a real, measured example, not an assumption.</p>
+<h4>Performance gate</h4>
+<p>Because this is a second model inference per person per frame, it does not run unconditionally. It only runs when the
+cheap 2D height signal already looks suspicious (<code>body_height_norm &lt; 0.85</code>) or is not yet calibrated at
+all — never on every normal standing frame in a live deployment.</p>
+<h4>Where it plugs in</h4>
+<p>Two places, both requiring a real geometric floor alongside the 3D reading (a lone 3D reading can never confirm by
+itself):</p>
+<ul>
+  <li><code>kneeling_penalty</code>: a confident <code>torso_angle_3d</code> reading of lying can relent the penalty
+  directly, independent of the 2D <code>torso_angle</code> reading.</li>
+  <li><code>fusion.py</code>'s new "confident 3D lying" path: confirms MANDOWN together with minimal geometric backing,
+  independent of the 2D-derived alternate path.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h3>5.8 V4.8.1 — Height-collapse requirement added to the 3D path <span class="tag tag-fixed">Kept</span></h3>
+<p><strong>Real trigger:</strong> the exact same deep-bend posture from §5.4/§5.5 reached MANDOWN again through the
+<em>new</em> 3D path, because a sufficiently deep bend genuinely tilts the torso in real 3D space too — measured live at
+<code>torso_angle_3d=0.777</code>, almost identical to its own 2D <code>torso_angle=0.778</code>. <code>torso_angle_3d</code>
+is not blind to a bend the way it is to a foreshortened fall, so a high reading alone does not distinguish the two.</p>
+<p><strong>Fix:</strong> require <code>height_collapse_cue</code> to also confirm a genuine collapse before trusting the
+3D path — a bend can never bring the hip down toward the floor (legs stay extended, hip stays at standing height),
+whereas any genuine fall does.</p>
+
+<h3>5.9 V4.9 — Torso-hip-angle-mismatch signal <span class="tag tag-open">Attempted twice; kept, with caveats</span></h3>
+<p><strong>Real trigger:</strong> even the height-collapse requirement above was not enough — under real per-camera
+calibration, a deep bend's overall bounding-box height <em>also</em> genuinely shrinks (head and shoulders come down
+close to hip level), so <code>height_collapse_cue</code> cleared its own floor too (measured: 0.53).</p>
+<p><strong>The real discriminator found:</strong> in a bend, the legs stay planted and near-vertical (low
+<code>hip_angle</code>) while only the torso hinges over (high <code>torso_angle</code>) — a large gap between the two.
+Genuine diagonal lying tilts the whole body as one unit, so <code>torso_angle</code> and <code>hip_angle</code> read
+close together (verified: ~0.02 gap on a diagonal-lying test fixture, vs. ~0.55 on the real bend).</p>
+<div class="box box-warn">
+<strong>First attempt — a scare, later resolved:</strong> checked against this project's single most protected real
+true-positive track using a <em>test script that did not wire in real per-camera height calibration</em>. That flawed
+check appeared to show the entire confirmed event suppressed to zero. The change was reverted immediately out of an
+abundance of caution. Re-verifying with the camera's <em>real</em> saved calibration wired in showed the actual
+confirming frames of that track have a <em>small</em> torso-hip gap (0.01–0.23) — the uncalibrated test had been
+misleading. The fix was re-applied after this correction, and the project's full test suite (250 tests) plus this
+targeted recheck both passed.
+</div>
+<p>This episode is recorded in detail because it illustrates the project's validation discipline in action: a change was
+reverted the moment a check — even an imperfect one — suggested regression risk to the most safety-critical case, and
+only re-applied after the check itself was corrected and re-verified.</p>
+
+<h3>5.10 V4.10 — Direct down_score confirm path <span class="tag tag-fixed">Kept — closed 4 gaps at once</span></h3>
+<p><strong>Real trigger:</strong> a person lay flat, foreshortened toward the camera, for a sustained 42 real seconds.
+The event log showed the fusion engine's own per-frame evidence read <code>suggested_tier: "MANDOWN"</code> with
+<code>down_score=0.925</code> (near-maximal) — yet the event never confirmed. The LSTM was 99.9% confident this was
+"normal" (<code>lstm_mandown_prob=0.001</code>) — the same 2D blind spot affecting the LSTM's own 5-feature input, since
+it is computed the same way.</p>
+<p><strong>Root cause:</strong> the existing "geometric-alone confirm" override (§3.4, rule 2) requires
+<code>feature_confidence ≥ 0.60</code> — but <code>feature_confidence</code> is a <em>blend</em> of
+<code>down_score</code> with several other components (including <code>fall_motion_score</code>, which correctly reads
+~0 for a static lying person) and is then discounted by <code>pose_quality</code>. In this real case the blend landed at
+0.602 — a hair above the threshold, not comfortably above it. Any small frame-to-frame dip broke the override,
+preventing the state machine's required run of consecutive confirming frames.</p>
+<p><strong>Fix:</strong> give <code>down_score</code> alone — already the single most direct "is this person down"
+signal, and already sufficient on its own to bypass the LSTM safety cap — a <em>direct</em> path to force the fusion
+score to the confirm threshold, instead of only reaching it indirectly through a blend that can dilute it.</p>
+<div class="box box-fixed">
+<strong>This is a monotonic improvement:</strong> it can only <em>raise</em> the fusion score for frames where
+<code>down_score</code> and persistence were already independently strong — it cannot lower the score for any case, and
+cannot admit any case that wasn't already permitted to bypass the safety cap by the pre-existing rule.
+As a side effect, it also closed three other long-standing, honestly-documented "known limitation" gaps in the test
+suite (diagonal lying, side-lying with bent knees, and the calibrated vertical fall with a cold LSTM) — all four shared
+the exact same root cause.
+</div>
+
+<h3>5.11 Ground-plane 2D perspective calibration upgrade <span class="tag tag-fixed">Kept</span></h3>
+<p>Independent of the fall-orientation fixes above: the scene calibration's perspective fit was upgraded from a 1-D
+(image row only) linear regression to a proper 2-D (row and column) fit. A camera mounted at an angle has perspective
+scale that genuinely varies across both axes, not just vertically — most visible on a wide-angle lens, where the old
+row-only fit's "expected size" was only correct along one column and increasingly wrong toward the frame edges.
+Degrades gracefully to the old row-only behavior when there isn't yet enough horizontal variation in the observed
+samples to fit a stable column term.</p>
