+<h2 id="limitations" class="section-break">7. Known Remaining Limitations (Honest Accounting)</h2>
+<p class="small">Per this project's standing discipline: a gap is reported honestly with real numbers, never quietly
+patched by loosening an unrelated threshold.</p>
+
+<h3>7.1 <span class="tag tag-open">OPEN</span> — Lying on the back with one knee raised</h3>
+<p>Most recently found. A person lay foreshortened toward the camera with one knee bent upward (hands clasped on chest) —
+the geometry differs enough from the flat-arms-folded posture in §6.2 that it did not confirm. Measured directly on the
+real frame:</p>
+<pre>torso_angle       = 0.121   (2D — near-zero, foreshortening blind spot as expected)
+hip_angle         = 0.175
+torso_angle_3d    = 0.319   (below the 0.55 "confident lying" floor — the raised knee likely
+                             shifts MediaPipe's inferred 3D shoulder/hip positions)
+down_score        = 0.454   (below the 0.65 direct-confirm floor)
+floor_proximity   = 0.552   (below the 0.70 floor the 2D alternate path needs)
+height_collapse   = 0.649   (clears its own floor, but no path currently uses it alone here)
+→ fusion capped at 0.45 (POSSIBLE_MANDOWN ceiling) regardless of LSTM confidence</pre>
+<p><strong>Diagnosis:</strong> none of the three "escape valves" (2D alternate path, 3D grounding path, direct
+down_score path) individually clears its own bar for this specific posture, even though every individual number is
+elevated in the correct direction. This is structurally the same class of problem as the V4.7 attempt (§5.6) — several
+moderately-elevated signals, no single one strong enough — and should <strong>not</strong> be patched by simply lowering
+one of the existing floors, for the same reason V4.7 was reverted: the same combination of moderate readings is also
+producible by postures that should not confirm. A safe fix needs a new, targeted discriminator specific to this posture
+(most likely: understanding why <code>torso_angle_3d</code> reads so much lower here than in the flat-arms case despite
+both being genuine lying-toward-camera postures — possibly the raised knee's keypoints are pulling MediaPipe's inferred
+hip position, or a per-posture MediaPipe accuracy limitation), analogous to how the hip-angle-mismatch signal (§5.9) was
+found for the bend case.</p>
+
+<h3>7.2 <span class="tag tag-open">OPEN — no safe algorithmic fix identified</span> — Inanimate object misdetected as a person</h3>
+<p>A dark bean-bag cushion on a couch is detected by the YOLO pose model as a "person" with moderate confidence (~0.63),
+and — once treated as a person — is reasonably (if wrongly) read as a collapsed posture by the downstream logic.</p>
+<p><strong>Why raising the detection-confidence threshold does not work:</strong> real confirmed falls in the reference
+videos are detected with confidence as low as 0.30–0.35 during parts of their own event (a collapsed, occluded, or
+unusually-posed real person is often harder for YOLO to confidently detect than this static object is). Any threshold
+high enough to reject the object would also reject real falls.</p>
+<p><strong>Why cross-checking with the new 3D lifting does not help either:</strong> tested directly — MediaPipe also
+confidently hallucinates a lying pose on the same object (<code>torso_angle_3d=0.89</code>, <code>evidence_quality=
+0.995</code>). Both models are pattern-matching plausible human shapes, not verifying "is this actually a human", so
+this cross-check adds no new information for this specific failure mode.</p>
+<p><strong>Recommended fix:</strong> physical — move, remove, or reposition the object (or reangle the camera slightly)
+so it no longer presents a person-like silhouette. No downstream scoring change can reliably separate "a real, oddly-posed
+person" from "an object that happens to look like one" using only the confidence signal available, since the two
+populations genuinely overlap.</p>
+
+<h3>7.3 <span class="tag tag-open">OPEN</span> — Camera 2, shallow-angle lying case</h3>
+<p>Found earlier in this engineering effort, prior to the 3D-lifting work: a person lying at a shallower angle than the
+near-zero foreshortened case, where every individual 2D/height signal reads moderate rather than strong. Documented at
+the time with exact numbers; not yet re-tested against the current, fuller feature set (2D+3D combined) — worth
+revisiting now that more discriminators exist.</p>
+
+<h3>7.4 <span class="tag tag-info">Environment, not code</span> — GPU clock throttling observed</h3>
+<p>During validation, the GPU was observed stuck at 180 MHz of a 3090 MHz maximum despite showing 100% utilization —
+an infrastructure/power-management issue unrelated to any change in this report, but worth investigating separately if
+real-time throughput becomes a concern in deployment, since it affects every video/live-stream run equally.</p>
+
